======================================================================================================================
1.  homogeneous sample: Milky Way classical satellites + isolated Local Group dwarfs
======================================================================================================================
  satellites N = 12: Draco, Ursa Minor, Sculptor, Sextans, Ursa Major, Carina, Hercules, Fornax, Leo V, Canes Venatici, Leo II, Leo I
  isolated   N = 10: Antlia B, Aquarius, Cetus, Leo A, Leo T, Pegasus dIrr, Phoenix, Sagittarius dIrr, Tucana, UGC 4879
  [PASS] A1 a homogeneous sample exists: the same photometric-to-mass pipeline, one stellar mass-to-light ratio, both environments represented with enough objects to fit a slope in each   (N_sat = 12, N_iso = 10, total 22)

======================================================================================================================
2.  THE EXPONENT: log sigma vs log M_bar -- is it 1/4?
======================================================================================================================
  log sigma = +0.460 + (0.075 +/- 0.017) log M_bar     [MOND structure predicts slope 1/4 = 0.250]
  [FAIL] A2 (THE EXPONENT) the slope of log sigma against log M_bar across all dwarfs is consistent with 1/4 -- the fourth-power law -- within its error   (slope 0.075 +/- 0.017, |slope - 0.25| = 10.4 sigma)
  with radius: log sigma = +0.301 + 0.066 log M + (+0.083 +/- 0.071) log r_h
  [PASS] A3 (THE MOND STRUCTURE) adding the half-light radius does NOT improve the relation: its coefficient is consistent with zero.  In Newtonian dynamics sigma^2 ~ G M/r, so radius must enter at -1/2 in log sigma; a relation that depends on M_bar ALONE is the signature of an acceleration-scale law, not a mass-in-a-radius law   (radius coefficient +0.083 +/- 0.071 (Newton would need about -0.5); scatter with/without radius 0.087 / 0.089 dex)

======================================================================================================================
2b.  WHERE the fourth-power law holds and where it breaks -- split by environment and by external field
======================================================================================================================
  The pooled slope is far from 1/4.  Before calling that a failure of the law, split the sample the way the physics
  says to: an EFE-dominated dwarf (external field > internal) cannot obey a baryon-only law in ANY MOND-like theory,
  because its dynamics are set by the host's field.  Compute g_ext/g_int for each and fit slopes per class.
  class                                     exponent test: slope of log sigma vs log M_bar (1/4 expected)
     isolated (no host)                 N = 10   slope +0.072 +/- 0.038   scatter 0.061 dex
     classical satellites (M_V < -8.2)  N =  9   slope +0.088 +/- 0.024   scatter 0.059 dex
     ultra-faint satellites             N = 3 (too few to fit)
     external field < internal (all)    N =  9   slope +0.126 +/- 0.043   scatter 0.045 dex
     external field > internal (all)    N = 13   slope +0.084 +/- 0.024   scatter 0.102 dex
  [FAIL] A2b (WHERE IT HOLDS -- and it does NOT hold even for the isolated dwarfs) restricted to dwarfs whose internal field exceeds the external one, the exponent is still far from 1/4.  The isolated dwarfs alone give a slope ~0.07, more than four sigma from the fourth-power law.  So the EXPONENT fails everywhere, not just where the external field dominates.  This corrects f14, which tested the COEFFICIENT at a fixed exponent of four and never fit the exponent itself   (isolated slope +0.072 +/- 0.038 (4.6 sigma from 1/4); field-dominated +0.126 +/- 0.043 (2.9 sigma))
  ⚠️ ONE SYSTEMATIC THAT PUSHES THE SAME WAY, stated: the gas-rich isolated dwarfs (Leo A, Pegasus, Aquarius, SagDIG)
  carry most of their baryons in a rotating HI disc, but sigma here is the STELLAR dispersion.  It under-counts
  kinetic energy carried in gas rotation, which flattens the relation at the gas-rich (high-M_bar) end.  A test that
  used the full baryonic kinetic energy could steepen the isolated slope; it cannot be run with these data.
  [PASS] A2c (AGAINST INTEREST, flagged post-hoc) the ultra-faint satellites are the objects that flatten the pooled relation: their dispersions are nearly constant across two decades of mass.  That is the known 'common mass scale' (Strigari+2008).  In this framework it is the external-field effect, which is a PREDICTION -- but the cut that recovers the law was chosen after seeing the pooled failure and must be quoted as such   (N<4)

======================================================================================================================
3.  THE COEFFICIENT: one number for satellites and isolated dwarfs?
======================================================================================================================
  canonical   C (satellites) median 10^-0.476 = 0.3341   C (isolated) median 0.0986   all 0.1679 scatter 0.637 dex   [theorem 4/81 = 0.0494]
  alt         C (satellites) median 10^-0.558 = 0.2768   C (isolated) median 0.0817   all 0.1391 scatter 0.637 dex   [theorem 4/81 = 0.0494]
  [FAIL] A4 (ONE COEFFICIENT) the coefficient is the SAME for satellites inside the Milky Way field and for isolated dwarfs outside it, within the error on the difference.  A law that does not know whether the dwarf orbits a giant is a law about the dwarf's own baryons   (satellites vs isolated: +0.530 +/- 0.229 dex (2.3 sigma))
  [FAIL] A5 (TIGHTNESS) the intrinsic scatter of the coefficient across 18 dwarfs in two environments is under 0.3 dex in C, i.e. under 0.075 dex in sigma -- tighter than the ~0.1 dex a halo population would put in   (total 0.637 dex in C; median error 0.235; intrinsic 0.569 dex in C = 0.142 dex in sigma)
  [PASS] A6 the coefficient is NOT the deep-MOND theorem's 4/81 -- it sits a factor ~2-3 above it on both footings -- so this is an EMPIRICAL law with an unexplained coefficient, which is exactly the epistemic status Kepler's third law had   (C_all/(4/81) = 3.40 (canonical), 2.82 (alt))

======================================================================================================================
4.  mutation controls
======================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M1 mutation: shuffling sigma among the galaxies destroys the slope and inflates the scatter, so the fourth-power relation is real structure   (shuffled slope +0.001, scatter 0.128 vs real 0.075, 0.089)
  [PASS] M2 mutation: the Newtonian-on-baryons combination sigma^2 r_h / (G M_bar) -- the mass-in-a-radius law -- is BOTH far from unity (the missing-mass excess) AND less tight than the acceleration law, so the data prefer the M_bar-only structure over the M-and-r structure   (Newtonian ratio median 10^+1.16 (factor 14 missing), scatter 0.670 dex vs 0.159 for the law in sigma)

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT
======================================================================================================================
  THERE IS NO DWARF M-SIGMA LAW WITH EXPONENT FOUR, and this file says so.  Pooled, the slope of log sigma on
  log M_bar is ~0.08.  Split by environment it is ~0.07 for the isolated dwarfs and ~0.09 for the classical
  satellites -- every subsample is more than four sigma from 1/4.  Dwarf velocity dispersions are nearly
  INDEPENDENT of baryonic mass across three decades: the common mass scale (Strigari+2008), and it is not confined
  to the external-field-dominated satellites.  The deep-MOND virial law sigma^4 = (4/81) G M_bar a_0 therefore
  fails on its EXPONENT, not merely its coefficient.
  THIS CORRECTS f14, which reported 'the scaling holds': f14 tested the coefficient at a fixed exponent of four and
  never fit the exponent.  Its 0.12 dex scatter was partly the exponent mismatch in disguise.  Cite f15, not f14,
  for the isolated-dwarf verdict.
  What survives: the relation is radius-free (A3) and Newton-on-baryons is worse (M2), so the data still prefer an
  acceleration-scale structure over a mass-in-a-radius one -- but with an exponent the theory does not predict.
  One systematic pushes the same way and is stated above (stellar sigma under-counts gas-disc kinetic energy).
  No Kepler-grade law here.  A real regularity (dispersions nearly mass-independent) that the framework does not
  produce.  Read with f09, f13, f14: pressure support breaks the kernel in exponent as well as amplitude.

